  "query_preview": "..A broad ridge is centered over the central United\nStates this evening, allowing for a southerly flow to develop over\nUtah and southwest Wyoming. The airmass over the area has remained\nrelatively moist, with satellite derived PW values in the 0.8 to\n1.0 range across much of the area, highest over southern Utah.\nEarlier showers and storms over southern Utah are decreasing in\ncoverage at this time. However, a series of weak shortwaves moving\nnorth across Utah overnight will allow for some showers to\ncontinue into the morning. \n\nThe southerly flow will allow moisture over the area to increase\nand spread northward through tomorrow afternoon, with PW values of\nan inch or more expected to spread over the vast majority of the\nforecast area. Combined with the supplemental instability from the\nshortwaves, this will bring another round of showers and\nthunderstorms, and any storms that develop will be capable of\nproducing heavy rain that could lead to flash flooding. The\nshowers and associated cloud cover area expected to keep high\ntemperatures near to just below seasonal normals during the day.\n\nBeginning Thursday, the broad ridge is expected to re-orient\nitself over the west, with the center moving directly over Utah\nfor the weekend. This will add a westerly component to the flow\naloft that will draw a drier airmass into the area, decreasing the\nthreat of convection. As the ridge strengthens, temperatures will\nbecome increasingly hot, peaking on Saturday with 700mb\ntemperatures expected to be in the lower 20s Celsius across the\nentire area. This will result very hot temperatures that will\nincrease the risk of heat related illness, particularly for valley\nlocations. Some uncertainty remains with regard to how high the\nHeatRisk will get on Friday, with Thursday nights lows potentially\ngetting low enough to keep the HeatRisk in the orange (moderate)\nrange for much of northern Utah during the day. For Saturday and\nSunday, confidence is higher than usual in the potential for very\nhot maximum temperatures, but there remains a good amount of\nspread in the ensembles with regard to low temperatures and\ntherefore how much overnight relief the area, northern Utah\nin particular, will see. Therefore, have decided to hold off on\nan Extreme Heat Watch at this time due to uncertainty about the\nlength of time of the event. \n\nBy early next week, the ridge is expected to weaken a bit and\nshift south as a Pacific Northwest trough moves over the top of\nit. This would provide some relief from the heat, at least for\nnorthern portions of the area, though there remains a good amount\nof spread in the guidance with regard to how much the trough will\nact to weaken the ridge and therefore how much of a drop in\ntemperatures there might be. It does seem likely, however, that\nthe very hot weather will continue for at least southeast Utah.",
